  "narrative": "During the 1990s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture flourished with a renewed emphasis on natural textures and traditional practices. The afro, a style created by combing out natural growth of kinky hair, remained a iconic representation of black identity and pride. Cornrows, a traditional hairstyle in many African cultures, were also popularized during this era. Box braids, another protective style, became a staple among Black Africans and the African diaspora, offering a versatile and low-maintenance option for styling textured hair. The natural hair movement, which emerged around this time, encouraged women to celebrate their inherent textures without chemical relaxers or heat-straightening. Dreadlocks, also known as dreads or locs, were formed naturally in very curly or kinky hair or created with techniques like twisting and backcombing. While the Jheri curl, a popular permanent wave hairstyle of the 1980s, may have still been worn by some during the early 1990s, its influence was waning as women began to reject chemical treatments and instead opt for natural hairstyles that celebrated their unique textures and identities.",
